<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Port Vila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Port Vila is a port town on the island of Efate and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>. In 1889 French and other settlers there declared the short-lived commune of Franceville, independent of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It later became the seat of the Anglo-French Condominium of the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and, from 1980, of independent Vanuatu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/port-vila.jpg" alt="Port Vila" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phillip Capper from Wellington, New Zealand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Vila_aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Port Vila lies on the west coast of the island of Efate, in Shefa Province, and is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>. Locally it is called simply Vila. The name of the area is Efil in the native South Efate language and Ifira in the neighbouring Mele-Fila language, of which Vila is a variant. Ifira is a small island in Vila Harbour where many traditional landowners live. The climate is tropical and maritime, with nearly constant trade winds, a wettest month in March and a driest in September.</p><p>Port Vila is the most important harbour and the centre of trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>. It is home to many languages: the daily <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> is Bislama, English and French are widely spoken, and many of Vanuatu's 138 indigenous languages are heard as people from rural areas move to the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Melanesian people have occupied the area for thousands of years. In 2004 an archaeological expedition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teouma">Teouma</a> found a burial site of 25 tombs holding about three dozen skeletons of members of the Lapita culture; ceramics from the site were dated to the 13th century BCE. James Cook charted Efate in 1774 and named it Sandwich Island. His expedition did not land, but noted the good harbours and land suitable for European settlement.</p><p>In the late 19th century, when the islands were known as the New Hebrides, the economic balance began to favour the French by the 1890s, and they established large plantations. The French citizen Ferdinand Chevillard bought and cleared land around Port Vila for what was to be the largest French plantation on the island; it became instead the municipality of Franceville, which declared independence on 9 August 1889 and lasted only until June of the following year. After 1887 the territory was jointly administered by the French and the British, formalised in 1906 as an Anglo-French Condominium. During the Second World War Port Vila was an American and Australian airbase.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Port Vila had 49,034 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, 16.3 per cent of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>. The people are predominantly Melanesian, with small Polynesian, Asian, Australian and European populations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> is the main religion in Vanuatu, and the city has a Roman Catholic cathedral, the Cathédrale du Sacré-Cœur, seat of the Diocese of Port Vila.</p><p>The Vanuatu Cultural Centre, which hosts the Vanuatu National Museum and the National Audiovisual Archives, stands at Saralana Park in front of the National Parliament. The city is one location of the University of the South Pacific, and is served by Bauerfield International Airport. It has been damaged by cyclones: Cyclone Uma in 1987 and Cyclone Pam on 13 March 2015, whose eye wall passed just east of the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Port Vila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1889 residents of the <b>Port Vila</b> area, mostly French settlers linked to the Société Française des Nouvelles-Hébrides, declared an independent municipality called Franceville.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-franceville">Republic of Franceville</a></li></ul></section>
